        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:</p>",
            "readable_text": "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>If we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x=0,]</span> we get a confusing result:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: lim_(x \u2192 0)(x^{3} \u2212 2x)/(x^{2} + 4x)|= ((0)^{3} \u2212 2(0))/((0)^{2} + 4(0)) = (0)/(0)]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "If we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting [MATH: x=0,] we get a confusing result:\n[MATH: lim_(x \u2192 0)(x^{3} \u2212 2x)/(x^{2} + 4x)|= ((0)^{3} \u2212 2(0))/((0)^{2} + 4(0)) = (0)/(0)]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The expression <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac 0 0]</span> is called an <strong>indeterminate form</strong> because we are unable to determine the value of the limit from this expression.</p>",
            "readable_text": "The expression [MATH: \\dfrac 0 0] is called an indeterminate form because we are unable to determine the value of the limit from this expression.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>However, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can factor the numerator and denominator and then cancel out any common factors.</p>",
            "readable_text": "However, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can factor the numerator and denominator and then cancel out any common factors.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}\n\n&amp; = \\dfrac{x(x^2-2)}{x(x+4)}\\[5pt]\n&amp; = \\dfrac{\\cancel{x}(x^2-2)}{\\cancel{x}(x+4)}\\[5pt]\n&amp;= \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4}\n}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}\n\n& = \\dfrac{x(x^2-2)}{x(x+4)}\\[5pt]\n& = \\dfrac{\\cancel{x}(x^2-2)}{\\cancel{x}(x+4)}\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4}\n}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Then, we take the limit of the simplified fraction.</p>",
            "readable_text": "Then, we take the limit of the simplified fraction.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} &amp;= \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\\n&amp; = \\dfrac{(0)^2-2}{(0)+4} \\\n&amp; = \\dfrac{-2}{4} \\\n&amp; = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} &= \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\\n& = \\dfrac{(0)^2-2}{(0)+4} \\\n& = \\dfrac{-2}{4} \\\n& = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nSuppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}]\n\nContent:\nIf we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting [MATH: x=0,] we get a confusing result:\n[MATH: lim_(x \u2192 0)(x^{3} \u2212 2x)/(x^{2} + 4x)|= ((0)^{3} \u2212 2(0))/((0)^{2} + 4(0)) = (0)/(0)]\n\nContent:\nThe expression [MATH: \\dfrac 0 0] is called an indeterminate form because we are unable to determine the value of the limit from this expression.\n\nContent:\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can factor the numerator and denominator and then cancel out any common factors.\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}\n\n& = \\dfrac{x(x^2-2)}{x(x+4)}\\[5pt]\n& = \\dfrac{\\cancel{x}(x^2-2)}{\\cancel{x}(x+4)}\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4}\n}]\n\nContent:\nThen, we take the limit of the simplified fraction.\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} &= \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\\n& = \\dfrac{(0)^2-2}{(0)+4} \\\n& = \\dfrac{-2}{4} \\\n& = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n}]"
      },
