    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nSuppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}]\n\nContent:\nIf we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting [MATH: x=0,] we get a confusing result:\n[MATH: lim_(x \u2192 0)(x^{3} \u2212 2x)/(x^{2} + 4x)|= ((0)^{3} \u2212 2(0))/((0)^{2} + 4(0)) = (0)/(0)]\n\nContent:\nThe expression [MATH: \\dfrac 0 0] is called an indeterminate form because we are unable to determine the value of the limit from this expression.\n\nContent:\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can factor the numerator and denominator and then cancel out any common factors.\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}\n\n& = \\dfrac{x(x^2-2)}{x(x+4)}\\[5pt]\n& = \\dfrac{\\cancel{x}(x^2-2)}{\\cancel{x}(x+4)}\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4}\n}]\n\nContent:\nThen, we take the limit of the simplified fraction.\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} &= \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\\n& = \\dfrac{(0)^2-2}{(0)+4} \\\n& = \\dfrac{-2}{4} \\\n& = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With One Quadratic Term by Factoring\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -3}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x+3}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nIf we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -3}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x+3} = \\dfrac{(-3)^2-9}{(-3)+3} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n}]\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n[MATH: (x^{2} \u2212 9)/(x + 3)|= (x^{2} \u2212 3^{2})/(x + 3); = ((x + 3)(x \u2212 3))/(x + 3); = (x \u2212 3).]\n\nTherefore,\n[MATH: lim_(x \u2192 \u2212 3)(x^{2} \u2212 9)/(x + 3)|= lim_(x \u2192 \u2212 3)(x \u2212 3); = \u2212 3 \u2212 3; = \u2212 6.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: lim_(x \u2192 \u2212 2)(2x + 4)/(x^{2} \u2212 3x \u2212 10).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(4)]\nb. [MATH: (7)/(2)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (7)/(2)]\nd. [MATH: (2)/(7)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (2)/(7)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: lim_(x \u2192 1) \u2212 (x^{2} \u2212 1)/(x \u2212 1).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 2]\nb. [MATH: 0]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 1]\nd. Does not exist\ne. [MATH: 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With Two Quadratic Terms by Factoring\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 6}\\dfrac{x^2-6x}{x^2-x-30}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nIf we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 6}\\dfrac{x^2-6x}{x^2-x-30} = \\dfrac{6^2-6\\cdot 6}{6^2-6-30} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n}]\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n[MATH: (x^{2} \u2212 6x)/(x^{2} \u2212 x \u2212 30)|= (x(x \u2212 6))/((x \u2212 6)(x + 5)) = (x)/(x + 5).]\n\nTherefore,\n\n[MATH: lim_(x \u2192 6)(x^{2} \u2212 6x)/(x^{2} \u2212 x \u2212 30)|= lim_(x \u2192 6)(x)/(x + 5); = (6)/(6 + 5); = (6)/(11).]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: lim_(y \u2192 2)(3y^{2} \u2212 12y + 12)/(y^{2} \u2212 4).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 0]\nb. [MATH: \u221e]\nc. [MATH: DNE]\nd. [MATH: (3)/(4)]\ne. [MATH: 3]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: lim_(x \u2192 4)(x^{2} \u2212 x \u2212 12)/(x^{2} \u2212 16).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 0]\nb. [MATH: (8)/(7)]\nc. [MATH: \u221e]\nd. Does not exist\ne. [MATH: (7)/(8)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function With Higher Order Terms by Factoring\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2+x}{x-1}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nIf we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2+x}{x-1} = \\dfrac{1^3-2(1)^2+1}{1-1} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n}]\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n[MATH: (x^{3} \u2212 2x^{2} + x)/(x \u2212 1)|= (x(x^{2} \u2212 2x + 1))/(x \u2212 1); = (x(x \u2212 1)^{2})/(x \u2212 1); = x(x \u2212 1).]\n\nTherefore,\n[MATH: lim_(x \u2192 1)(x^{3} \u2212 2x^{2} + x)/(x \u2212 1)|= lim_(x \u2192 1)x(x \u2212 1); = 1(1 \u2212 1); = 0.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: lim_(y \u2192 \u2212 3)(y^{3} + 6y^{2} + 9y)/(2y^{2} \u2212 2y \u2212 24).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (3)/(14)]\nb. [MATH: 0]\nc. [MATH: \u221e]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 \u221e]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (3)/(14)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: lim_(y \u2192 0)(y^{3} + 2y^{2})/(y^{2} + 3y).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (2)/(3)]\nb. [MATH: 2]\nc. [MATH: \u221e]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 \u221e]\ne. [MATH: 0]"
  }
